endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (In this manual, we sometimes refer to "a generation" just for simplicity. However, the dates associated with them are not precise boundaries and do not mark sudden changes in behaviour or attitudes. In reality, young people even within the same generation can differ greatly depending on culture, country, socio-economic background, education, family context, and personal interests. These terms simply help us to engage in discussions about them and the social trends that arrive, change or disappear over time because of them. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (It is helpful to treat generational descriptions as starting points for curiosity rather than labels.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 138 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [1468 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (To better understand the new generations, we explored existing research and collected insights from youth workers, trainers, educators, and young people themselves. Before diving into the findings, it is important to note that terms like Gen Z and Gen Alpha are just broad reference points. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
