>>
endobj
860 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (One of the strongest messages from practitioners in recent research is simple: "we need to listen more". Young people often tell us exactly what they need - sometimes in words, sometimes in behaviour, sometimes in silence. Instead of seeing presumed and/or observed shorter attention spans as a problem to fix, you can treat them as an invitation to reflect on how your own attention span has changed. Staying in tune with new generations begins with listening as a continuous practice, not a one-off activity. This is less about collecting feedback forms and more about cultivating a mindset of curiosity toward young people's voices, behaviours, and realities. When you listen with openness rather than defensiveness, you learn far more about the changing generations than any report alone can teach you. Think of listening as part of your professional growth:  )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
861 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (This section invites you to reflect on your role, your methods, and your mindset. It is an invitation to grow and evolve alongside the young people you work with. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
